Project Overview: Structured RAG Hotel Search Pipeline
This project demonstrates how to build an end-to-end Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline using LangChain to bridge natural language queries with a relational SQL database. Instead of searching unstructured text documents, the system translates user intents into precise database queries to fetch live inventory and availability data.

Core Components & Workflow
Retrieval Phase (retrieve_knowledge)

Schema Inspection: Utilizes LangChain's database utility to understand tables (hotel, hotel_room, room_reservation) and their relationships.

Text-to-SQL Conversion: Takes the user's natural language request (e.g., searching for hotels with hot springs in Tokyo for specific dates) and generates an executable SQLite query.

Execution & Guardrails: Validates the generated SQL for safety, queries the database, and returns the structured records as a Pandas DataFrame.

Augmentation Phase (format_results_for_llm)

Converts the resulting tabular DataFrame into a clean, LLM-readable string format (to_string(index=False)).

Binds dynamic parameters (such as the original user query and schema templates) to prompt templates using partial variable injection.

Generation Phase (generate_response)

Passes the retrieved hotel data and constraints through a structured prompt template instructing the model to adopt a welcoming, structured tone.

Outputs a formatted response highlighting hotel names, locations, unique features (e.g., natural hot springs), room pricing tiers, and capacity details.
